{
  "case_id": "2005-04-473",
  "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "CINDY S. LEE (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. LEE: Chief Justice Roberts, and may it please the Court: The issue presented is whether the first amendment requires protection for all public employee speech that touches on a matter of public concern without any consideration of whether the speech was expressed as a citizen."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Counsel, you've made the point that if we go the Ninth Circuit way, every time an employee gets in Dutch there's a potential first amendment issue. Why hasn't that been a problem since 1988 in the Ninth Circuit? I think 1988 was the year of the Circuit's Roth decision. So, we haven't seen a deluge, and doesn't that rather discount your argument?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Is that --\nMS. LEE: -- I'd like to --\nJUSTICE SOUTER: -- is that true in this case?\nI realize they didn't get to it on summary judgment, but\nis that true in this case with respect to a Brady\ndisclosure?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Well, does that mean -- what's\nyour answer to my question? If he makes the Brady\ndisclosure because he believes that is an ethical\nobligation, and he is then subject to retaliation, does he\nhave a first amendment claim, or not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus)",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0001-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HIMMELFARB: A Brady obligation is an\nobligation of a prosecutor in his capacity as a\nprosecutor; and a Brady disclosure, like a recommendation\nto a superior that there should be a Brady disclosure,\nconstitutes the exercise of the prosecutorial function by\na prosecutor. It is employee speech, and that speech, if it\nengenders an employment action, should not be sufficient\nfor the employee to get past the first step of the Pickering\nbalancing."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR: So, no free-speech protection\nunder Pickering."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What if the reason -- what if the reason for the -- for firing him is that he's a\nDemocrat and it's a Republican Administration, and the speech is used as the pretext?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: But, Mr. Himmelfarb, in that case he could also be fired if he made the statement in a speech, could he not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: I suppose you have the rule which distinguishes between employment-related and outside speech, under the hypothetical -- difficult hypothetical posed by the Chief Justice, it would be an -- the promise would be an unconstitutional condition, or something like that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Ms. Lee -- Ms. Lee told us that the Ninth Circuit weighed the capacity of the plaintiff as an employee, rather than a member of the public, in favor of the employee and against the employer. Is that how you read the Ninth Circuit's decision?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "My question to you is, What standard does that judge apply under the first amendment? What does he do?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. Robin-Vergeer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The judge looks first at whether the speech is on a matter of public concern. And if it's a dispute over Government misconduct, it would meet that threshold."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What if he does let the employer try first, and the employer does nothing, then he goes public? Where does the Pickering balance come out then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. Robin-Vergeer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Court held, in both Givhan and Rankin, that private communications on matters of public concern are still protected."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. Robin-Vergeer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "a report of Government misconduct by an employee to his supervisor, at a minimum, should be treated as meeting whatever threshold the Court establishes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robin-Vergeer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "there's this extra element present here, which is that there is an independent constitutional problem here, in that when you have a -- police misconduct, you have someone whose right to fair trial are at stake, and you have a prosecutor who's trying to fulfill his individual ethical and constitutional obligation --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Is that your answer?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robin-Vergeer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Almost all these cases go to summary judgment. It's"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robin-Vergeer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The whistleblower statutes, which are sort of a patchwork nationwide, protect, or at least they have the ability to protect, speech beyond what the first amendment does."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robin-Vergeer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0003-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Whistleblower statutes are patchwork across the country. Some would cover this kind of speech, some would not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lee",
      "case_id": "2005-04-473",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fbdd39657ad03f47844b565985c3a451688f1372e686bb0a0f331b25322debd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fbdd39657ad-0003-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under our approach, we believe that many cases won't even be filed, because they won't be able to make a colorable claim that it is citizen speech."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
